  "notes": "A spatial broadcast timetable. Two bus registers of N words each, a row bus and a column bus, carry one outer-product slice per cycle: start loads column 0 of A and row 0 of B onto them straight from the inputs and puts the rest of A and B into two shift registers (A >> W, B >> N*W); in each busy cycle the buses take the next column of A and the next row of B from those registers. An N x N array of stationary output cells reads the buses: in each of the N busy cycles cell (i, j) adds row-bus word i times column-bus word j (both sign-extended, wrapping at AccW) to its accumulator, which start clears. A 32-bit step counter ends the busy phase after N steps and raises done (latency N). It differs from the shift-register reference in that the cells read registered row and column buses, loaded from the inputs at start, not the heads of the shift registers. The design is parametric in N, W and AccW. The design is the player's file, byte for byte. The proof is the player's file with parts the maintainers wrote and handed over, roughly half of its lines: the cell step arithmetic, the queues' names and shape, the commit, bus and busy-step lemmas, reset and start, and the whole last part (the protocol phases, the x-safety lemmas and the `solution` theorem, under the comment that marks it as the maintainers' reserve).",
  "player": "clockwork-otter",
